{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1789342054,"nonce":"6K5x86X1qRPnLbg7EPTQTER/vjc=","message":"Round: the remaining cheap levers, each cell alone, ns per call, median of 5 runs of 200k (baselines Haxe 3.6, Wren 3.6–5.8).\n\nWren→Haxe: static 52.5 → 40, method 57 → 44, getter 31.7 → 24, setter 33 → 30, closure 61 → 50.\nHaxe→Wren: static 26.2 → 25.5, method 26.4 → 24.5, getter 14.7 → 14.3, setter 19.5 → 19.6.\nConstruct and the Haxe→Wren closure are allocation-bound and move with the machine's load (construct 268–350 Haxe→Wren, 300–350 Wren→Haxe; closure 55–95); the machine was under an XProtect scan for part of this round.\n\nWhat landed:\n- wren_lift b2f7257 (issue 69d5fe7 there): a `wren_call_N` send reads its JIT state once (frame stack folded into `JitThread`, VM taken from the state, threaded through the frameless pass and `dispatch_call_rooted`); a host method the method cache resolves in the frameless pass runs there, under its frame, with no second lookup; IC kind 8 for host methods, dispatched inline by the mod.rs backend (the Cranelift backend encodes no IC index unless `WLIFT_ENABLE_JIT_CALLSITE_IC`, so there the gain is the frameless pass); `call_found_closure` reads `disabled` from the state it holds and restores only the context fields it writes.\n- ash 4fa9393 (issue 508b63e there): `hlp_remove_trap_in(storage)` pops a caller-armed trap through the state the context recorded when armed, one thread-local access per call instead of two.\n- caribou b6f53f1: the bridge's `direct` answers `Result\u003cValue, ()\u003e`, so the Ok path builds and drops no `Outcome`; the shim calls `hlp_remove_trap_in`; the bench prints the run's collection count under `WLIFT_TIER_STATS`.\n\nWhere a Wren→Haxe static call's 40 ns goes now (Ash's sampler, self time):\n- the trap, about 10: `_setjmp` 3, the shim 2.7, `hlp_setup_trap_in` 2 plus its one thread-local read 1, `gc_lock_held_depth` through the seam 1.8, the remove 0.7;\n- caribou-ash, about 10: `call_by_kinds` 6, `direct_typed` 1.6, `dispatch_by_pattern` 1.8, the thunk 0.7;\n- caribou-wren, about 9: `host_entry` with `run` inlined 5.7, `call_at_opt` 1.8, `to_wren` 1.3, `cross_out` 0.7;\n- wren_lift, about 7: `wren_call_1_inner` with the frameless pass inlined 4.7, the compiled caller 1.6, one thread-local read 1.\n\nA Haxe→Wren static call's 25.5: `entry` with `run` inlined 5, `call_found_closure` 4.5 plus `modvars_cell_addr` 0.7, `direct_call` 4, `call_at_opt` 3.3, two thread-local reads 3 (`VM_HERE`, the JIT state), `cross` 1.5, `to_wren` 0.9, `finish` 0.5, the compiled body 0.7.\n\nWhat is left, and why the +10 ns target is out of reach without a design change:\n- The trap is a quarter of Wren→Haxe and cannot go below ~7 ns as a setjmp: HashLink raises by longjmp, so every call into Haxe code from a frame that must not be jumped over needs its own jump buffer. Arming one per Wren entry instead of per call would abandon wren_lift's frames on a throw. Only an Ash exception model that unwinds through foreign frames (or a call convention where a throw returns a code) removes it.\n- The glue on each side (`host_entry`/`entry`, `call_by_kinds`, `direct_call`) is ~10 ns per side of small branches: kind switches, argument conversion, result conversion, the `Result` plumbing. Calling the site's direct send straight from the adapter's slot would skip `call_at_opt` (~2 ns per side). A record-held thread token in place of `VM_HERE` saves one thread-local read (~1.5).\n- Haxe→Wren's `call_found_closure` (~5) is the cost of finding compiled code and swapping context per call; a per-site cache of the compiled entry would need re-validation against `jit_code` per call, as wren_lift's IC kind 1 does.\n- Construct makes three objects (the Wren instance, its `WrenRef`, the Haxe face) and a handle, under two `REFS` mutex operations and the GC lock, and a dropped ref costs a map removal and a deferred handle release at the next lock release. A ref slot in the Wren object's prefix would remove the map, not the allocations; the floor is the allocation of three objects.\n- The Haxe→Wren closure call allocates the var-args array `hlp_make_var_args` packs per call; typed callbacks (`#export = \"f: Fn(Num) -\u003e Num\"`) are the fix, deferred.","files":null}]}